board_modern_avr — every supported modern AVR, in one board
============================================================

Where board_atmega4809 and board_attiny3217 are per-family demos, this board is
the *coverage* board: it proves that **every** modern core this fork supports
boots and exercises its core peripherals. That is all 23:

  tinyAVR 1-series (15): attiny212 214 412 414 416 417 814 816 817
                         1614 1616 1617 3214 3216 3217
  megaAVR 0-series (8):  atmega808 809 1608 1609 3208 3209 4808 4809

Each core is checked against seven peripherals present identically on every
modern part: PORT, TCA0, TCB0, RTC, ADC0, SPI0 and TWI0 (USART0 is the result
transport, so it is covered too).

How it works
------------

  * blink_fw.c is a single device-agnostic firmware. It brings up all seven
    peripherals through the avr-libc device-header structs (TCA0, TCB0, RTC,
    ADC0, SPI0, TWI0, USART0) — identical across both families — and every loop
    builds a result bitmask (one bit per peripheral that responded) and streams
    it on USART0. It uses only PA0 and bounded waits, so one source builds and
    runs unchanged from the 8-pin ATtiny212 to the 48-pin ATmega4809 and a
    non-responding model can never hang the run. The Makefile compiles it once
    per device into fw_<device>.axf, passing the device name in MCU_NAME so each
    ELF's .mmcu section names the right core.

  * runner.c (the host) loads each fw_*.axf, instantiates the core named in its
    .mmcu section, attaches a tiny I2C slave at 0x50 (so the firmware's TWI0
    transaction completes), runs ~200 ms of simulated time, and ORs the result
    bytes. A core PASSes only if every required peripheral bit appears. It prints
    a per-peripheral line (NAME+ responded / NAME- did not) and exits non-zero if
    any core fails.

Build & run (from this folder)
------------------------------

Open a console, change into this folder, build, then run every core:

    cd examples/board_modern_avr   # the runner loads fw_*.axf from here
    make                           # builds the runner + fw_<device>.axf per core
    make run                       # runs the runner over every fw_*.axf

Expected output (one line per device, then a summary):

    PASS  attiny3217   PORT+ TCA0+ TCB0+ RTC+ ADC0+ SPI0+ TWI0+
    ...
    23/23 cores PASSED

To run just one device, call the host runner with that firmware:

    ./obj-*/runner.elf fw_attiny3217.axf      # a single core
    ./obj-*/runner.elf fw_atmega4809.axf fw_attiny412.axf   # a chosen few

The runner binary is built under obj-<arch>/ (e.g. obj-x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/
runner.elf); the ./obj-*/ glob picks it regardless of architecture.

Toolchain note
--------------

Building firmware needs a modern avr-gcc (12+) with the tinyAVR-1 / megaAVR-0
device headers (verified with avr-gcc 16). The Makefile filters the device list
down to the cores the installed avr-gcc can actually target, so a plain `make`
never fails on an older toolchain — it just builds fewer (or no) firmwares, and
the host runner builds regardless. `make run` then exercises whatever fw_*.axf
are present (the .axf are build artifacts, not committed — see .gitignore).

Adding a new modern device
--------------------------

Drop its sim_<device>.c in simavr/cores/ (per the auto-discovery in
simavr/Makefile), then add its avr-gcc name to ALL_MCUS in this Makefile. No
firmware edits are needed — blink_fw.c is device-agnostic.
